00:08:54,360 --> 00:08:57,120
现在，事情开始于一点点自我舒缓，
Now, what starts off as a little bit of self soothing,

176
00:08:57,230 --> 00:08:59,650
马上这些物体表现出非常奇怪的性质，
soon these objects take on very strange qualities,

177
00:08:59,760 --> 00:09:01,050
几乎它们都是活的。
almost as if they're alive.

178
00:09:01,170 --> 00:09:04,920
孩子们甚至和它们说话，他们认为它们也会有感情。
Children even talk to them. They think they've got feelings.

179
00:09:05,040 --> 00:09:06,790
他们让它们变得几乎和人一样，
They make them almost humanlike,

180
00:09:06,900 --> 00:09:09,530
当你思考这件事时会觉得很不可思议
which is extraordinary when you think about it

181
00:09:09,640 --> 00:09:12,190
因为这只是块布。
'cause it's just a piece of cloth.

182
00:09:12,310 --> 00:09:14,100
Bruce想知道
Bruce wanted to find out

183
00:09:14,210 --> 00:09:17,360
为什么孩子们认为这些物品很特殊。
why children believe these objects are so special.

184
00:09:17,480 --> 00:09:19,500
然后他进行了一项实验
So, he performed an experiment

185
00:09:19,610 --> 00:09:22,270
用的是小孩们和一个魔法机器。
with young kids and a magic machine.

186
00:09:22,380 --> 00:09:24,100
Bruce告诉孩子们
Bruce told the children

187
00:09:24,220 --> 00:09:27,170
这个机器可以完美复制他们的玩具，
it would make a perfect copy of their toys,

188
00:09:27,290 --> 00:09:31,410
实际上，这要感谢一个藏在里面的研究者。
thanks, in fact, to the help of a hidden researcher.

189
00:09:31,520 --> 00:09:35,740
他接下来告诉孩子们他们只能拿走一个玩具
He then told them they were allowed to keep only one toy

190
00:09:35,860 --> 00:09:38,110
另一个就必须扔掉。
and must throw away the other.

191
00:09:38,230 --> 00:09:41,350
几乎每个孩子都选了之前的
Nearly every child chose the original

192
00:09:41,460 --> 00:09:43,080
而丢弃了复制品。
and tossed the copy.

193
00:09:43,200 --> 00:09:45,090
他们想要回之前的玩具。
They needed the original one back.

194
00:09:45,200 --> 00:09:48,460
我想这是因为他们以一种实在论的方法思考。
And I think it's because they're thinking in an essentialist way.

195
00:09:48,570 --> 00:09:50,590
这是一种我们用额外维度
This is an idea that we view the world

196
00:09:50,700 --> 00:09:52,190
审视世界的观点。
with this additional dimension.

197
00:09:52,300 --> 00:09:55,060
实在主义是一种信仰
Essentialism is the belief

198
00:09:55,170 --> 00:09:58,460
说的是一个确定的物体具有隐藏的属性，
that certain objects have a hidden essence,

199
00:09:58,580 --> 00:10:01,130
而这种属性不能通过复制而转移
one that cannot be transferred to a copy

200
00:10:01,250 --> 00:10:04,270
即使外观看起来非常像。
even if it looks absolutely identical.

201
00:10:04,380 --> 00:10:09,510
这是小孩子们强烈拥有的坚定的信仰。
It is a conviction young children hold strongly.

202
00:10:09,620 --> 00:10:13,910
但是我们因为年纪大了而不具备这种隐藏属性感了吗？
But do we outgrow this sense of a hidden essence?

203
00:10:14,020 --> 00:10:17,480
Bruce将演讲转为一个实验找到了一个答案，
Bruce found an answer by turning his lectures into an experiment,

204
00:10:17,590 --> 00:10:19,150
也是他今天试图在
one that he's trying out today

205
00:10:19,260 --> 00:10:23,950
英国科学研究所人员身上找到的答案。
on the staff of the Royal Institution.

206
00:10:24,070 --> 00:10:26,920
去年我在纽约，我买了支爱因斯坦--
I was in New York last year, and I bought Einstein --

207
00:10:27,030 --> 00:10:28,990
爱因斯坦的一支钢笔。
one of Einstein's fountain pens.

208
00:10:29,100 --> 00:10:31,490
这是爱因斯坦用过的笔。
So this is an original Einstein pen.

209
00:10:31,600 --> 00:10:33,860
对此我很骄傲，如果你们想看看。
I'm very proud of this, if you like to have a look.

210
00:10:33,970 --> 00:10:36,810
事实上，很欢迎你们能够拿着它传给别人看看。
In fact, you're welcome to hold it and pass it along.

211
00:10:43,250 --> 00:10:46,570
现在，我又碰巧有另一件东西。
Now, I happen to have another thing here.

212
00:10:46,680 --> 00:10:48,940
这是件羊毛衫。
This is a cardigan.

213
00:10:49,050 --> 00:10:50,940
它属于Jeffrey Dahmer，
It belonged to Jeffrey Dahmer,

214
00:10:51,050 --> 00:10:53,740
谋杀了17个人的连环杀手，
the serial killer who murdered 17 people

215
00:10:53,860 --> 00:10:56,310
残忍地将被害者分尸并吃了他们，
and brutally cut them up, ate them,

216
00:10:56,430 --> 00:10:58,950
还做些非常卑劣的事。
and did some very despicable things.

217
00:10:59,060 --> 00:11:02,360
那么现在你们是否会拿着它互相传递么？
Now, would you like to pass that along and hold it?

218
00:11:02,470 --> 00:11:03,470
里面的这件羊毛衫？
The cardigan itself?

219
00:11:03,590 --> 00:11:04,990
是的。你会穿上它么？
Yeah. Would you put it on?

220
00:11:05,100 --> 00:11:08,190
-我不会穿的，不会的。 -不会？
-I won't put it on, no. -No?

221
00:11:08,300 --> 00:11:11,260
大多数人会因这个毛衣是连环杀手
Most of us are revolted by the thought

222
00:11:11,370 --> 00:11:13,930
穿过的而感到厌恶，
of wearing the sweater of a serial killer,

223
00:11:14,040 --> 00:11:17,110
不论它被清洗过多少次。
no matter how many times it may have been cleaned.

224
00:11:19,480 --> 00:11:23,270
好的，让我来带你们走进一个秘密。
Well, I have to let you into a secret.

225
00:11:23,380 --> 00:11:25,170
这完全不是Jeffrey Dahmer的毛衣。
It's not Jeffrey Dahmer's sweater at all.

226
00:11:25,280 --> 00:11:28,370
但是仅仅因为想到这会是连环杀手的衣服，
But just the thought of it belonging to the serial killer,

227
00:11:28,490 --> 00:11:31,010
对大多数人来说都会感到反感厌恶。
for most people, it's repulsive and repugnant.

228
00:11:31,120 --> 00:11:33,680
那么很难过得告诉你们，这并不是爱因斯坦的笔。
And, sad to say, this isn't Einstein's pen.

229
00:11:33,790 --> 00:11:35,090
这只是个普通的钢笔。
It's just a regular fountain pen.

230
00:11:37,190 --> 00:11:39,650
Bruce认为这种宗教感
Bruce believes this sense

231
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:41,450
以及邪恶的本质感
that sacred and evil essences

232
00:11:41,570 --> 00:11:43,720
可以影响到物质世界
can contaminate the material world

233
00:11:43,830 --> 00:11:48,890
而且这些感觉是人类灵性中最初的形式，
is the most primal form of human spirituality,

234
00:11:49,010 --> 00:11:53,300
也是每个宗教能够建立的基础。
a foundation upon which every religion is built.

235
00:11:53,410 --> 00:11:55,630
我认为宗教正是利用了这种假想
I think religions capitalize on this assumption

236
00:11:55,740 --> 00:11:57,000
使人们认为有一种隐藏的结构。
that there is hidden structure.

237
00:11:57,110 --> 00:11:59,570
宗教做的，就是提供一个框架，
What religions do is they provide a framework,

238
00:11:59,680 --> 00:12:01,240
一个故事，能让人们
a narrative which allows people

239
00:12:01,350 --> 00:12:04,420
试着将这些力量以一种意味深长的方式组合起来。
to try and put these forces together in a meaningful way.

240
00:12:08,750 --> 00:12:11,480
像Bruce这样的心理学家认为
Psychologists like Bruce argue

241
00:12:11,590 --> 00:12:14,150
天生的精神直觉
that this innate spiritual intuition

242
00:12:14,260 --> 00:12:16,780
可能是我们智慧思维的人工制品
might be an artifact of our intelligent minds

243
00:12:16,900 --> 00:12:20,420
试图理顺我们混乱的世界。
trying to make sense of a chaotic world.

244
00:12:20,530 --> 00:12:23,490
但这种天生隐藏在精神维度中的信仰
But this innate belief in a hidden spiritual dimension

245
00:12:23,600 --> 00:12:26,890
经常会被一些体验而加强，
is often reinforced by experience,

246
00:12:27,010 --> 00:12:31,560
因为我们10个人中就有一个会看到这个神秘的领域
because one in 10 of us will visit this mysterious realm

247
00:12:31,680 --> 00:12:34,530
这是一种出体体验。
in an out-of-body experience.

248
00:12:34,650 --> 00:12:38,350
当我们离开我们的肉体，我们会看到神么？
When we leave our bodies, do we meet God?

249
00:12:40,800 --> 00:12:45,160
是否会有另一个真实的世界而我们无法看到？
Is there a reality beyond the world we see?

250
00:12:45,430 --> 00:12:48,980
一个神和灵魂居住的世界？
A place where God and spirits live?

251
00:12:49,100 --> 00:12:53,320
一些人相信他们看过这个隐藏的世界
Some people believe they have glimpsed this hidden world

252
00:12:53,430 --> 00:12:55,590
通过出体经验。
in an out-of-body experience.

253
00:12:55,700 --> 00:13:00,400
这些现象都能证明神的存在吗？
Are these phenomena proof of the existence of God?

254
00:13:03,670 --> 00:13:08,130
神经病学家Olaf Blanke一直尝试发现
Neurologist Olaf Blanke is trying to discover

255
00:13:08,250 --> 00:13:12,900
在经历出体体验时候到底发生了什么。
what really happens during an out-of-body experience.

256
00:13:13,020 --> 00:13:16,110
对于出体现象非常典型的
What is very typical for this out-of-body phenomenon

257
00:13:16,220 --> 00:13:17,740
就是它的感觉。
is that it is felt.

258
00:13:17,850 --> 00:13:19,580
是一种很高程度的精神体验。
It's highly spiritual.

259
00:13:19,690 --> 00:13:20,840
考虑一下。
You know, think about it.

260
00:13:20,960 --> 00:13:22,310
你觉得自己分开了。
You feel separated.

261
00:13:22,420 --> 00:13:24,280
你的思维实际上--
Your mind is physically --

262
00:13:24,390 --> 00:13:27,250
或是说你的感觉离开了你的肉体。
or you experience as separated from your body.

263
00:13:27,360 --> 00:13:29,490
那么，这怎么可能呢？不符常理。
So, how could this be? This doesn't fit.

264
00:13:31,460 --> 00:13:34,020
大多数经历过出体体验的人
Most people who have had an out-of-body experience

265
00:13:34,130 --> 00:13:39,090
都报告说自己的精神到了一个隐藏的国度。
report being spirited away to a hidden realm.

266
00:13:39,210 --> 00:13:42,630
但是Olaf怀疑这些经历
But Olaf suspects these voyages to the beyond

267
00:13:42,740 --> 00:13:45,640
纯粹发生在我们的脑袋里。
take place purely inside our heads.

268
00:13:46,580 --> 00:13:48,200
这因为当他在诊断治疗
Because while diagnosing

269
00:13:48,310 --> 00:13:51,370
他的一个癫痫患者时，
one of his epileptic patients for treatment,

270
00:13:51,480 --> 00:13:53,000
向她大脑中植入的电极
he sent a mild current

271
00:13:53,120 --> 00:13:55,740
发射了轻微电流
to electrodes implanted in her brain

272
00:13:55,850 --> 00:14:00,740
不经意间就引发了出体体验。
and inadvertently triggered an out-of-body experience.

273
00:14:00,860 --> 00:14:02,410
她还记得
She had the impression

274
00:14:02,530 --> 00:14:04,880
她刺激治疗室的
that she herself would be under the ceiling

275
00:14:04,990 --> 00:14:08,450
天花板下面往下看，
of the stimulation room and to be looking down,

276
00:14:08,560 --> 00:14:10,620
看着她自己，她的身体，
seeing herself, her body,

277
00:14:10,730 --> 00:14:12,800
还有坐在她周围的人们。
as well as the people sitting around her.

278
00:14:15,040 --> 00:14:19,160
Olaf还通过微弱电流
Olaf had sent a mild stimulating current

279
00:14:19,270 --> 00:14:23,460
刺激病人的颞顶交界处，即TPJ。
to his patient's temporoparietal junction, or TPJ.

280
00:14:23,580 --> 00:14:25,500
这种身体的自动描绘场景现象，
This automatized body representation,

281
00:14:25,610 --> 00:14:28,340
就是当我们刺激这个病人特定区域时，
when we stimulate it in this region in this one patient,

282
00:14:28,450 --> 00:14:29,940
不能将你
could not fuse

283
00:14:30,050 --> 00:14:32,610
从哪里看到你的身体和你从哪里感觉到你的身体融合。
where you see your body and where you feel your body.

284
00:14:32,720 --> 00:14:36,140
而这类不连贯的表现
And this kind of discoherent representation

285
00:14:36,250 --> 00:14:39,220
就会导致出体体验。
may lead to an out-of-body experience.

286
00:14:40,790 --> 00:14:43,550
TPJ（颞顶交界）是大脑的导航者，
The TPJ is the brain's navigator,

287
00:14:43,660 --> 00:14:46,020
就像一艘潜水艇的舰长
rather like the captain of a submarine

288
00:14:46,130 --> 00:14:49,090
他看不到他的潜艇朝向哪里
who can't actually see where his vessel is heading

289
00:14:49,200 --> 00:14:52,760
但可以通过间接测量自己的位置，
but has to rely on indirect measurements of his position,

290
00:14:52,870 --> 00:14:55,870
比如测量水压和使用声纳。
like water-pressure readings and sonar pings.

291
00:14:57,970 --> 00:15:01,500
如果进入TPJ的信息受到干扰
If the data coming into the TPJ is interfered with,

292
00:15:01,610 --> 00:15:05,230
那么导航系统就会混乱。
the navigation system can become disoriented.

293
00:15:05,350 --> 00:15:09,340
TPJ可以告诉你你被颠倒了
The TPJ could tell you you are upside down

294
00:15:09,450 --> 00:15:11,680
或是你位于哪里（但实际不是）。
or somewhere you really aren't.

295
00:15:16,020 --> 00:15:18,910
如果他的假说是正确的，
If his hypothesis was correct,

296
00:15:19,020 --> 00:15:23,280
Olaf认为出体体验
Olaf realized that out-of-body experiences

297
00:15:23,390 --> 00:15:26,950
可以通过干预人们的感觉，
might also be induced in any brain, epileptic or not,

298
00:15:27,060 --> 00:15:29,650
在任何大脑中诱发。
by tampering with people's senses.

299
00:15:29,770 --> 00:15:32,890
为了在一个健康未植入电极的受试者身上
And to achieve this effect in a healthy subject

300
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:34,930
实现这种效应，
without implanting electrodes,

301
00:15:35,040 --> 00:15:39,700
Olaf建立了一个高端的虚拟现实实验室。
Olaf built a cutting-edge virtual-reality laboratory.

302
00:15:39,810 --> 00:15:43,640
虚拟现实使得我们可以在实验室中
Virtual reality gives us the possibility in the research lab

303
00:15:43,750 --> 00:15:48,380
让受试者的触感和视觉分离。
to disassociate touch from sight of our participant's body.

304
00:15:48,500 --> 00:15:50,820
小心。
Careful.

305
00:15:50,930 --> 00:15:53,260
那么对于这个实验嘛，
So, for this experiment,

306
00:15:53,370 --> 00:15:55,890
你要站在那里，脚放在那前面。
you will stand with your feet in front of there.

307
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:57,430
嗯~对。
Mm-hmm. Yes.

308
00:15:57,540 --> 00:16:00,100
然后我会把这个放在你头上，
Then I will put this on your head,

309
00:16:00,210 --> 00:16:02,730
然后你就要看屏幕上的指示。
and you will follow instructions on the screen.

310
00:16:02,850 --> 00:16:05,300
这名受试者通过放置在她身后的
The subject sees a live feed

311
00:16:05,410 --> 00:16:07,950
摄像机中看到直播影像。
from a camera placed behind her back.

312
00:16:09,850 --> 00:16:12,570
她感到背上有轻柔的抚摸感，
She feels a gentle stroke on her back,

313
00:16:12,690 --> 00:16:14,980
但是同时她看到她的身体
but sees the stroking as if her body

314
00:16:15,090 --> 00:16:17,750
比实际上的靠前两英尺。
is actually two feet in front of her mind.

315
00:16:17,860 --> 00:16:20,810
这样大脑就面临着一个空间上的矛盾，
So the brain is exposed to a spatial conflict,

316
00:16:20,930 --> 00:16:22,830
而随着这种空间矛盾
and being exposed to this spatial conflict

317
00:16:22,940 --> 00:16:23,940
持续足够长的时间，
for a long time,

318
00:16:24,050 --> 00:16:26,950
人们就会认为这个替身是自己的身体
people start identifying with the avatar

319
00:16:27,070 --> 00:16:29,790
而不是他们自己真正的身体。
rather than with their physical body.

320
00:16:29,900 --> 00:16:33,760
当我们要求受试者
When asked to move back from where they are standing

321
00:16:33,870 --> 00:16:36,130
回到他们之前站的地方，
and then return to the same spot,

322
00:16:36,240 --> 00:16:40,300
他们并不会回到原来的位置，
the subjects always end up not in their original position,

323
00:16:40,410 --> 00:16:42,770
而是靠前两英尺--
but two feet forward --

324
00:16:42,880 --> 00:16:46,140
精确地回到他们看到的视觉替身的位置。
precisely where their virtual avatar appeared to be.

325
00:16:46,250 --> 00:16:47,340
这种感觉--
The feeling is --

326
00:16:47,450 --> 00:16:49,770
一开始非常奇怪，
it's very strange at the beginning,

327
00:16:49,890 --> 00:16:51,770
但实际上感觉很好。
but it's actually very pleasant.

328
00:16:51,890 --> 00:16:55,310
这更像我的思维--
It's more like my mind --

329
00:16:55,420 --> 00:16:58,980
我的身体并不只是我看到的一个，
like my body is more the one I'm seeing,

330
00:16:59,090 --> 00:17:01,180
到底哪个才是我的身体，
which is actually my body,

331
00:17:01,300 --> 00:17:05,620
不过更像是我看到的那个。
but it's more like I'm there where I see myself to be.

332
00:17:05,730 --> 00:17:08,920
当Olaf在一名身上穿着E.E.G.感应器的
When Olaf performed this experiment

333
00:17:09,040 --> 00:17:12,130
受试者进行这项实验时，
with the subject wearing an E.E.G. sensor,

334
00:17:12,240 --> 00:17:15,800
他发现大脑的颞顶交界出
he discovered that the brain's temporoparietal junction

335
00:17:15,910 --> 00:17:18,000
非常活跃。
was highly active.

336
00:17:18,110 --> 00:17:22,030
TPJ在努力克服感觉输入的冲突
The TPJ was struggling to create a cohesive reality

337
00:17:22,150 --> 00:17:25,140
来创造真实的现实，
out of the conflicting sensory input,

338
00:17:25,250 --> 00:17:27,240
而最终结果就是
and the net result was

339
00:17:27,350 --> 00:17:31,540
她产生了不在自己肉体内的感觉。
the sensation that she might be outside her body.

340
00:17:31,660 --> 00:17:35,880
Olaf认为当我们出现出体经历时，
Olaf believes that when we have an out-of-body experience,

341
00:17:35,990 --> 00:17:37,880
我们从未离开过我们的身体，
we never leave our bodies,

342
00:17:37,990 --> 00:17:39,580
我们看到的那些
and the entities we sense

343
00:17:39,700 --> 00:17:43,220
无非是大脑产生的幻象。
are nothing more than phantoms of the brain.

344
00:17:43,330 --> 00:17:47,790
